                <pb facs="0860" n="758" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg758"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ministry for Foreign
                            Affairs to the American
                    Embassy.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Sublime Porte, Ministry for Foreign Affairs</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1908-09-26"><hi rendition="#italic">September
                                26, 1908</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Note verbale: In reply to the note verbale which the American Embassy kindly
                    addressed to the ministry for foreign affairs, dated 13th of August last,
                    numbered 1024, the ministry for foreign affairs has the honor to inform the
                    embassy that, according to a communication of the imperial ministry of the
                    interior, since the reestablishment of the constitution, all subjects of the
                    Empire being free to travel abroad, there is no need to request the imperial
                    authorities of Chemisguzek to permit Mrs Marie Dirabian and her daughter to join
                    their husband and father, residing in the United States.</p>
            </div>
